## §3. Structural Findings

### F1 — The SoftLoop traverses edges its own kernel declares illegal

`kernel/lifecycle.py` is a ratified reference monitor. Tested against it, the live loop's stage
sequence contains **three illegal edges**:

| Edge | `is_legal(..., SUBSTANTIAL)` | Layer skipped |
|---|---|---|
| `RE_DERIVE → IDEATE` | **False** | L01 `INTAKE`, L02 `TRIAGE` (as stages) |
| `TRADEOFF → DESIGN` | **False** | **L07 `MITIGATION`** |
| `ADVERSARIAL_REVIEW → SEAL` | **False** | **L15 `LEDGER_SYNC`** |

All other executed edges are legal.

### F2 — Nothing enforces this, because the orchestrator never proposes a transition

`Gate.propose_transition` (`kernel/gates.py:99`) is the four-tuple reference monitor and it *does*
consult `is_legal` (`:130`). Its only caller is the interactive CLI (`cli/paeos.py:206`).
**`runtime/orchestrator/__init__.py` never calls it.** The autonomous loop dispatches stages
directly through `StagePlaybook.run_stage` and appends a single `goal_created` event.

This is the root enabler of F1: layers can be omitted silently because no component checks the
walk. The three illegal edges are not the *cause* of the gap — they are the *symptom* that the
kernel gate is bypassed on the autonomous path. **Any candidate in §4 that lands a layer without
routing it through the gate reproduces the exact defect that allowed the layer to go missing.**

### F3 — Four "layer" modules exist that are pure vacuum

`runtime/mitigation.py` (9 lines), `runtime/critique.py`, `runtime/retrospect.py`,
`runtime/evolve.py` (8 lines each) are byte-identical in shape: one unused
`from kernel.types import ArtifactRef`, one function whose body is `pass`, and a docstring naming
the layer.

For L09/L17/L18a the real behavior lives elsewhere (`ROLE_SPECS` + orchestrator wiring), so those
stubs are merely dead. **For L07 the stub is the whole implementation** — `runtime/mitigation.py`
is the sum total of L07 in this repository.

This is the empirical failure mode any construction plan must be designed against:

> **A canonical layer is instantiated when it is dispatched in `_run_body` with a `RoleSpec`, an
> artifact obligation, and a kernel gate — never when a module bearing its name exists.**

The presence of `runtime/mitigation.py` will make L07 *look* built to a shallow audit. It is not.
A completion claim citing that file is a false positive, and `06_COMPLETENESS_CRITERIA.md` is the
correct rebuttal.

### F4 — L08 DESIGN runs with an unmet canonical entry condition on every single run

`operations/ENGINEERING_LIFECYCLE.md:116` — **"L08 Entry: selected approach (L04/L06) +
mitigations (L07)."**
`spec/PAEOS-9-execution-architecture.md:729` — L07 on failure **"blocks L08."**

The loop currently feeds `DESIGN` a trade-off matrix with no mitigation catalogue. Every design
this system has produced was produced under an entry condition the constitution declares unmet.
This is not a latent risk; it is a standing violation on every historical run.

### F5 — The debt ledger is a live stale-ledger incident (the L15 failure mode, already realized)

`ledger/debt/README.md` indexes **25 entries**, DEBT-0001 through DEBT-0025, with descriptions and
statuses. `ls ledger/debt/` returns **`README.md` and nothing else**. A repo-wide
